{
  "rule": "Where each TypeScript compiler labelled edge lands in our tree. The node map says the same things are there; this says they are CONNECTED the same way.",
  "forms": "`[\"right\"]` -- the oracle's child IS the child under our `right` field. `[\"body>\"]` -- it lives somewhere UNDER our `body` field, which is what a list field looks like when we wrap it. `[]` -- we attach this child positionally, with no field name. `[\"=\"]` -- the oracle's child has the SAME span as its parent, so there is no edge of ours to label: tsc's `TypeReference.typeName` on a bare `Foo` points at `Foo` itself. Distinct from `[]`, which says the child is a real separate node we left unnamed.",
  "review_note": "Started from what the corpus showed and then CORRECTED, because the observation is contaminated by parent-span collision: several of our nodes can share one span, so the fields of an ENCLOSING node get attributed to the inner one. Taken as generated, `ExpressionStatement.expression` would have been declared to be our `body` or `consequence` field, and `TypeReference.typeName` to be `constraint` or `return_type`. Both are false; both are the kind of thing a table built from observation quietly enshrines. Every entry naming a semantically loaded field -- left/right, condition/consequence/alternative, name/alias, key/value, object/property -- was read by hand.",
  "unlabelled_note": "More than half the entries are `[]`. An unnamed edge is one a consumer cannot query by role, so the count is a queue rather than a formality: list members, modifiers, and the single child of a wrapper are the bulk of it.",
